<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jolof Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jolof, Wolof Empire</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jolof Empire was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a> state in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> that dominated the region from the fourteenth to the sixteenth century. It was made up of a confederation of kingdoms under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buurba-jolof">Buurba Jolof</a>. It has its own profile on the site, and broke apart after its coastal provinces rebelled.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jolof-empire.jpg" alt="Jolof Empire" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cheikh Khadim Rassoul D. · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Jolof_Empire.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The region was first inhabited by the Soce and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a>, whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a> had driven south by the 13th century, and it was ruled by Lamanes of the Mbengue, Diaw and Ngom families. Jolof was long a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>. Wolof tradition names as founder the possibly mythical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndiadiane-ndiaye">Ndiadiane Ndiaye</a>, a stranger who appeared from a lake, settled a dispute among the Lamanes and was made king, after which the rulers between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a> rivers submitted to him. Historians place the rise of the state in the 14th century, perhaps during a succession dispute within the Mali Empire around 1360.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The empire absorbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayor">Cayor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baol">Baol</a> early, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Sine</a> and an early Saloum in the late 14th century at the earliest, completing its core with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waalo">Waalo</a>. Portuguese ships reached the coast from the 1440s, and horses bought from them may have helped expansion. Around the 1450s Buurba Biram Njeme Eler conquered Namandirou, extended his authority over Takrur and moved the capital to Thieung. After his death five buurbas claimed the throne between roughly 1455 and 1489, sowing civil war.</p><p>In the 1480s Buumi Jelen, ruling for his brother, moved the seat of government to the coast for the Portuguese trade; opponents murdered the buurba in 1489, and Jelen fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, was baptised, and was killed by the commander of the force King John II sent to restore him. The coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> then took most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic trade</a>'s wealth. In 1513 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koli-tenguella">Koli Tenguella</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/futa-toro">Futa Toro</a>, in 1520 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Sine</a> and Saloum broke away, and in 1549 Amari Ngoone Sobel Fall of Kayor defeated Jolof at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-danki">Battle of Danki</a>, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waalo">Waalo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baol">Baol</a> also left.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Buurba was chosen by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> who included the rulers of the five main constituent kingdoms, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waalo">Waalo</a>, Kayor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baol">Baol</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Sine</a> and Saloum, each of which chose its own ruler, kept practical autonomy and sent tribute. Society was divided into royal and other nobles, free men, occupational <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> such as smiths and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griots">griots</a>, and slaves. The Lingeer, head of all women, owned tribute-paying villages and was influential in politics. Trade ran through weekly markets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, salt, beans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, with iron bars and cloth serving as money.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jolof Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Without access to the coast, <b>Jolof</b> was poorer than Cayor or Waalo and was often drawn into their wars.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-jolof">Kingdom of Jolof</a></li></ul></section>
